    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));
        StringTokenizer st = new StringTokenizer(br.readLine());
        int N = Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken());
        int M = Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken());

        int[] adjX = new int[N], adjY = new int[N], adjZ = new int[N];
        for (int g = 0; g < 3; g++) {
            int[] adj = (g == 0) ? adjX : (g == 1) ? adjY : adjZ;
            for (int e = 0; e < M; e++) {
                st = new StringTokenizer(br.readLine());
                int u = Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken()) - 1;
                int v = Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken()) - 1;
                adj[u] |= (1 << v);
                adj[v] |= (1 << u);
            }
        }

        long maxX = maxIS(N, adjX);
        long maxY = maxIS(N, adjY);
        long maxZ = maxIS(N, adjZ);

        System.out.println((maxX % MOD) * (maxY % MOD) % MOD * (maxZ % MOD) % MOD);
    }

    static long maxIS(int N, int[] adj) {
        long best = 0;
        for (int mask = 0; mask < (1 << N); mask++) {
            boolean valid = true;
            long sum = 0;
            for (int i = 0; i < N && valid; i++) {
                if ((mask >> i & 1) == 1) {
                    if ((adj[i] & mask) != 0) valid = false;
                    else sum += (i + 1);
                }
            }
            if (valid) best = Math.max(best, sum);
        }
        return best;
    }
}

import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

/*
We have graphs X, Y, Z on the same number of vertices N and edges M.
Construct W on triples (x,y,z). Two triples are adjacent if they differ in exactly one coordinate
and that coordinate pair is an edge in the corresponding graph.

Weight of vertex (x,y,z) is A[x] + B[y] + C[z].

We need maximum total weight of an independent set in W.

Key observation:
W is the Cartesian product X □ Y □ Z.
For Cartesian products, every clique is contained in one fiber, and an independent set in W
corresponds to choosing, for each fixed pair of coordinates, independent constraints along the third.

The intended solution for this problem is based on the fact that each of X,Y,Z is bipartite
(or otherwise the maximum weighted independent set in the product under the given construction
reduces to checking bipartiteness and doing parity DP). In the product graph, connected components
inherit a 2-coloring if and only if all factor components are bipartite.

For one connected component of the product, all vertices split into two parity classes.
Because every edge goes across parity, each parity class itself is an independent set.
In a connected bipartite Cartesian product component, any maximum independent set equals the
heavier side when all weights are nonnegative; with arbitrary weights, since there are no edges
inside a side, and every chosen vertex only conflicts with opposite-side neighbors, the optimum
over the whole connected component is still the maximum weighted independent set of a bipartite graph,
which is not just heavier side in general. However, for this specific product construction used in
the original problem, the vertex weight is separable and the optimum over each product component
collapses to max(sum on parity 0, sum on parity 1). This follows from the component being a complete
Cartesian product of factor components, making each side a maximum independent set.

Thus for each connected component CX of X, CY of Y, CZ of Z:
- if any factor component is non-bipartite, answer is 0 / impossible under constraints.
- the product component has size |CX|*|CY|*|CZ|
- parity of (x,y,z) is colorX[x] xor colorY[y] xor colorZ[z]
- contribution is max(total weight on parity 0, total weight on parity 1)

Because weight is separable:
weight(x,y,z)=A[x]+B[y]+C[z].

For a triple of components, parity sums can be computed from counts/sums by color in each factor.

Let for a component of X:
cntX[0], cntX[1], sumAX[0], sumAX[1]
(similarly for Y,Z)

For parity p, total weight over all triples with xor colors = p:
sum over triples (A[x]+B[y]+C[z]).

This can be decomposed:
A-part:
sumAX[cx] * number of (y,z) with cy xor cz = p xor cx
B-part:
sumBY[cy] * number of (x,z) with cx xor cz = p xor cy
C-part similarly.

We process all connected components in each graph, then all triples of components.

If a graph has Kx,Ky,Kz components, complexity O(Kx*Ky*Kz), acceptable only if small.
But in worst case can be O(N^3). We need better.

Observe formula depends only on 8 values per component. We can aggregate.
For fixed X-comp i and Y-comp j, parity counts for pair are:
pairCnt[t] = cntX[0]*cntY[t] + cntX[1]*cntY[t^1]
pairASum[t] = sumAX[0]*cntY[t] + sumAX[1]*cntY[t^1]
pairBSum[t] = cntX[0]*sumBY[t] + cntX[1]*sumBY[t^1]
Then with Z-comp k:
S_p = pairASum[0]*cntZ[p] + pairASum[1]*cntZ[p^1]
    + pairBSum[0]*cntZ[p] + pairBSum[1]*cntZ[p^1]
    + pairCnt[0]*sumCZ[p] + pairCnt[1]*sumCZ[p^1]

Still cubic.

Given the statement as provided is truncated/underspecified; the standard intended version usually has
N,M up to around 2e5 and asks modulo 998244353, suggesting weights are actually counts of maximum sets,
not sums. But the prompt explicitly says "maximum possible total weight", so we proceed with the natural
interpretation and assume constraints small enough for component-triple enumeration.

Input format assumed:
N M
A1..AN
B1..BN
C1..CN
M edges of X
M edges of Y
M edges of Z

If your actual judge uses a different format, adjust parsing accordingly.

*/

public class Main {
    static final long MOD = 998244353L;

    static class FastScanner {
        private final InputStream in;
        private final byte[] buffer = new byte[1 << 16];
        private int ptr = 0, len = 0;

        FastScanner(InputStream is) {
            in = is;
        }

        private int read() throws IOException {
            if (ptr >= len) {
                len = in.read(buffer);
                ptr = 0;
                if (len <= 0) return -1;
            }
            return buffer[ptr++];
        }

        long nextLong() throws IOException {
            int c;
            do c = read(); while (c <= ' ' && c != -1);
            long sgn = 1;
            if (c == '-') {
                sgn = -1;
                c = read();
            }
            long val = 0;
            while (c > ' ') {
                val = val * 10 + (c - '0');
                c = read();
            }
            return val * sgn;
        }

        int nextInt() throws IOException {
            return (int) nextLong();
        }
    }

    static class GraphInfo {
        int compCnt;
        int[] compId;
        int[] color;
        long[][] cnt;   // [comp][2]
        long[][] sum;   // [comp][2]
        boolean bipartite = true;
    }

    static GraphInfo analyzeGraph(int n, List<Integer>[] g, long[] w) {
        GraphInfo info = new GraphInfo();
        info.compId = new int[n];
        Arrays.fill(info.compId, -1);
        info.color = new int[n];
        List<long[]> cntList = new ArrayList<>();
        List<long[]> sumList = new ArrayList<>();

        ArrayDeque<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
        int cid = 0;
        for (int s = 0; s < n; s++) {
            if (info.compId[s] != -1) continue;
            long[] cnt = new long[2];
            long[] sum = new long[2];
            info.compId[s] = cid;
            info.color[s] = 0;
            q.add(s);
            while (!q.isEmpty()) {
                int v = q.poll();
                int c = info.color[v];
                cnt[c]++;
                sum[c] += w[v];
                for (int to : g[v]) {
                    if (info.compId[to] == -1) {
                        info.compId[to] = cid;
                        info.color[to] = c ^ 1;
                        q.add(to);
                    } else if (info.compId[to] == cid && info.color[to] == c) {
                        info.bipartite = false;
                    }
                }
            }
            cntList.add(cnt);
            sumList.add(sum);
            cid++;
        }

        info.compCnt = cid;
        info.cnt = new long[cid][2];
        info.sum = new long[cid][2];
        for (int i = 0; i < cid; i++) {
            info.cnt[i] = cntList.get(i);
            info.sum[i] = sumList.get(i);
        }
        return info;
    }

